The OOC library (oo2c 1.5.9, by Michael van Acken, Michael Griebling and others; LGPL, see the
module headers), as voc has it (src/library/ooc), for polpo. In polpo the modules have no ooc
prefix, except where polpo has a module of that name: oocStrings, oocFiles, oocRandomNumbers.
voc keeps the prefix (oocIntStr); vocsync translates the imports of the shared modules.

Packages (arden base):
  ooc-strings      Ascii, CharClass, oocStrings, Strings2, OakStrings
  ooc-math         LowReal, LowLReal, RealMath, LRealMath, ComplexMath, LComplexMath, OakMath,
                   oocRandomNumbers
  ooc-conversions  ConvTypes, IntConv, IntStr, LongInts, RealConv, LRealConv, RealStr, LRealStr
  ooc-time         SysClock, JulianDay; Time on x86
  ooc-io           x86: Msg, Channel, FilesHost, oocFiles, ProgramArgsHost, ProgramArgs,
                   TextRider, BinaryRider, Filenames
Time and ooc-io use type-bound procedures: the x86 compiler takes them with /2; the ROP2 back
ends (RISC-V, MIPS, ARMv7) cannot call them and the ARM compiler (OB) does not know them.

Written for polpo (LGPL too, as the library they belong to):
  SysClock         gettimeofday of Linux0
  FilesHost        the host of oocFiles on Files of polpo: a handle is a file and a position;
                   plain names are in the current directory; Truncate copies the file
  ProgramArgsHost  the arguments of the command (Oberon.Par), the command name first
Changed for the polpo compilers: FOR loops are WHILE loops and read-only exports (-) are *
(the ARM compiler); LowReal uses the Reals of polpo; LRealMath builds its constants with
Reals.RealL (no HUGEINT); small in LowReal and LowLReal is a quotient of powers of two (the
polpo compilers refuse 1.17549435E-38 and 2.2250738585072014D-308); Filenames has no Rts.
Fixed here and in voc (2026-10-06): sincos lost the sign of cos; REAL tan, sin and cos reduced
with REAL pi; REAL arcsin and arccos gave wrong results after any earlier error; oocLowReal
scale, intpart, trunc and round took a REAL for a 64 bit SET.
